{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties","id":1072953,"title":"Hacking Untrained Penalties","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-04-03T22:36:44-07:00","id":1072953,"title":"Hacking Untrained Penalties","content":"I have never liked the Untrained Penalty of skills with no dots in them. In particular, I find the massive -3 to mental skills a bit too much. Has anyone tinkered with these and can tell me how it worked?<br /> <br /> I have specifically been thinking about 2 hacks:<br /> 1) Making all skills without dots have just a -1 penalty, including mental skills. I don't know, I just don't find it convincing that a person with 3 Intelligence would get a chance die a Investigation unless trained..<br /> 2) Getting rid of Untrained Penalty altogether. If players want to roll an untrained skill they just roll their Attributes instead. In this case no dots just mean you have no special training but are simply average. If the player REALLY wishes to be especially bad at something they may get an &quot;Incompetent&quot; Condition to simulate this.<br /> <br /> How have other people thought about this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":9849,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9849-aristarkos","user":"Aristarkos","date":". 04-04-2017, 01:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9849-aristarkos","name":"Aristarkos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9849_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-03T22:58:42-07:00","id":1072959,"title":"","content":"Actually, I think I'd split the difference and make it a -2 Penalty for all untrained skills, regardless of whether it was Mental, Physical, or Social if I were to make any changes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2309,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","user":"Ashenrogue","date":". 04-04-2017, 02:01 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-03T23:00:55-07:00","id":1072960,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't drop the untrained penalty completely, mostly because its existence encourages generalized characters over specialized ones; the difference between 0 dots and 1 dot is more significant than the difference between 4 dots and 5 dots, so there is more inscentive to put at least 1 dot in more skills than there is to put a lot of dots in one or two skills. If this doesn't bother you, feel free to disregard this argument.<br /> <br /> However, I don't disagree that the -3 penalty for untrained mental skills as opposed to the -1 penalty for other untrained skills does smack of old-fashioned game design. I can't see anything that extra -2 does that improves the game, beyond a very dubious nod to &quot;realism&quot;. And honestly, the idea that being untrained in a mental task is more of a detriment than being untrained in a physical or social task has more to do with intellectual elitism than it does with actual reality.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 04-04-2017, 02:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-03T23:05:40-07:00","id":1072961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1072959#post1072959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, I think I'd split the difference and make it a -2 Penalty for all untrained skills, regardless of whether it was Mental, Physical, or Social if I were to make any changes.</div> </div> </div> </div>I could get behind that change. That would make the difference between untrained and trained roughly equivalent to one expected success.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T00:00:37-07:00","id":1072976,"title":"","content":"It think it's worth considering a few things:<br /> <br /> 1) Intelligence 3/Investigation 0 isn't automatically a chance die. It's a base dice pool of 0. A character can spend Willpower, have some equipment, and end up with good odds of a success. A -3 isn't a meaningless penalty, but it's not the end of the world either. The untrained penalties really end up being more, &quot;if you want to roll this, you're going to have to spend some resources to make it work,&quot; than, &quot;you shouldn't bother because even though you can roll, it's pointless.&quot;<br /> <br /> 2) With the 2e beat economy, higher untrained penalties mean more chance-die, means more dramatic failures, and more Beats. Having a few Skills you suck at can be a decent Beat source without having yet another Condition to track (and as a different kind of reward means you can double them up).<br /> <br /> 3) The &quot;nod to verisimilitude&quot; is something I'd weigh a bit. It's not really that important, especially if it bothers you, but for plenty of people it does add to the experience. I'd definitely consider <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2309_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2309\" data-vbnamecard=\"2309\">Ashenrogue</a> here over nothing if you want the penalty to be even across Skills. As alluded to in point 1, it's not that hard to get a decent dice-pool even with a penalty. I think there's a big risk of ending up effectively adding untrained penalties back in the game via situational penalties being applied for what amounts to no training so that you don't have Attribute 2/Skill 0 characters having ~85-90% odds of success on a roll they're willing to throw WP at and isn't resisted or contested. I think it's very likely that verisimilitude will come back in anyway with things like, &quot;-2 situational penalty for using a tool you've never seen before.&quot;<br /> <br /> The Condition option isn't horrible, but unless it's one Condition that covers all your zeroes, it would get extremely clunky.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T03:30:25-07:00","id":1073017,"title":"","content":"Another hack could be to tie it to you Primary, Secondary, and Tertiary skill groups. Primary is -1, Secondary -2, Tertiary -3.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T04:43:57-07:00","id":1073031,"title":"","content":"You try hacking or operating someone without knowledge in a programming syntax or basic anatomy and lemme know how that goes, k?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T05:03:18-07:00","id":1073040,"title":"","content":"The problem is that some actions (surgery and hacking) should have hefty unskilled penalties while others (the entire Investigation Skill) shouldn't be more difficult that any of the other Skill categories.<br /> One way to deal with it is to have -1 unskilled penalties and add additional penalties if you don't have the correct (or just a relevant) Specialty. If I remember correctly you need a Heavy Weapons Specialty on Firearms or get a -2 penalty to use certain weapons such as flamethrowers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T09:38:30-07:00","id":1073190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073031#post1073031\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You try hacking or operating someone without knowledge in a programming syntax or basic anatomy and lemme know how that goes, k?</div> </div> </div> </div>You are 100% correct in your implication, someone without knowledge would have a much harder time. But, just as Tessie brought up, this is something I would rather adjust myself with penalties based upon just what is being done in the situation.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9849-aristarkos","name":"Aristarkos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9849_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T10:56:45-07:00","id":1073252,"title":"","content":"While I can understand the desire to &quot;just do it yourself,&quot; keep in mind that this means your players can start to suffer a lot of unfun uncertainty around such things (and thus avoid them instead of engaging with fun story options). That's sort of the whole point of having rules instead of ST fiat for the game. A set penalty from the books is something known, and something the players can feel agency towards approaching. If &quot;untrained&quot; is changed to a, &quot;0 to -5 penalty at ST discretion,&quot; players have to take those hits with only their experience with the ST as a guide for what they're in for.<br /> <br /> As well, keep in mind that as the ST, you have extremely strong control over situational modifiers as it is. If you want an Investigation roll to be something even an untrained person has a good shot at... you can give it a +3 for a bazillion reasons (though I'm not quite sure about the assumption that Investigation is something that is over penalized, things like cryptography and forensics aren't exactly something people can do with ease, and you can let players make perception or memory/trivia checks instead of full Investigations in many situations where the penalty might seem too high).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T11:14:01-07:00","id":1073264,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073252#post1073252\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As well, keep in mind that as the ST, you have extremely strong control over situational modifiers as it is. If you want an Investigation roll to be something even an untrained person has a good shot at... you can give it a +3 for a bazillion reasons (though I'm not quite sure about the assumption that Investigation is something that is over penalized, things like cryptography and forensics aren't exactly something people can do with ease, and you can let players make perception or memory/trivia checks instead of full Investigations in many situations where the penalty might seem too high).</div> </div> </div> </div>Isn't that proposal? Just roll unskilled modifiers into the general situational modifers?<br /> <br /> That would even allow a degree of consistency in giving skill based modifers for other ratings aside from zero. I mean, to use Malus' example, someone trained in first aid, isn't going to be much more equiped for performing an operation than the unskilled person. I mean, as is, the core describes the various dot ratings in highly differentiated ways which a +1 mechanic doesn't really do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T19:37:13-07:00","id":1073508,"title":"","content":"I can perform all the physical and social skill actions either without any training or a few minutes of instruction. I don't care if my intelligence, etc. were increased to 10, I still would not be able to translate Arabic, hack a computer, rebuild an automotive engine, or perform open heart surgery. I'm going to need more than a 5 minute tutorial to have any chance to succeed.<br /> <br /> For the easier mental skill actions, why doesn't the character automatically succeed? I'm sure there are instances where neither of the standard options fit, so ad hoc bonuses to mitigate the penalty would work at those times. I think the situations that might call for non-standard solution are much more rare than the other two, so I don't see a need to change the rule for the rare cases by making the more common situations to no longer fit.<br /> <br /> I don't care so much about the specific numbers as I think that falls into subjectivity, but I agree with the untrained should be more difficult and mental significantly more difficult than physical or social. Honestly, I consider the easier mental skills as an excuse not to bother rolling. &quot;Let's see how many dots in Investigation you have. As I thought, you automatically succeed.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T20:54:31-07:00","id":1073546,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073264#post1073264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Isn't that proposal? Just roll unskilled modifiers into the general situational modifers?</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a suggestion yet, but I'm generally against it for reasons specified.<br /> <br /> As an additional issue, it makes it very hard to separate &quot;situational penalty for this being a specifically hard task,&quot; and, &quot;free form untrained penalties as situational modifiers.&quot;<br /> <br /> Though that's sort of the point I was going for anyway. You can make a task's situational modifier +3 because it's just easy. But that +3 goes for everyone equally, rather than having to be adjusted for each player's traits.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-04T21:15:25-07:00","id":1073553,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Diggs</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073508#post1073508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>I can perform all the physical and social skill actions either without any training or a few minutes of instruction. I don't care if my intelligence, etc. were increased to 10, I still would not be able to translate Arabic, hack a computer, rebuild an automotive engine, or perform open heart surgery. I'm going to need more than a 5 minute tutorial to have any chance to succeed.</b><br /> <br /> For the easier mental skill actions, why doesn't the character automatically succeed? I'm sure there are instances where neither of the standard options fit, so ad hoc bonuses to mitigate the penalty would work at those times. I think the situations that might call for non-standard solution are much more rare than the other two, so I don't see a need to change the rule for the rare cases by making the more common situations to no longer fit.<br /> <br /> I don't care so much about the specific numbers as I think that falls into subjectivity, but I agree with the untrained should be more difficult and mental significantly more difficult than physical or social. Honestly, I consider the easier mental skills as an excuse not to bother rolling. &quot;Let's see how many dots in Investigation you have. As I thought, you automatically succeed.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, you'd have no chance of doing the super high technical aspects of the mental skills but that applies to the highest form of any of the skills. You'd have about as much luck performing a complex acrobatic routine as you would open heart surgery given no experience in Athletic activites and only a 5 minute explanation on proper form.<br /> <br /> That said, having 10 in an Attribute <i>might</i> let you get away with it. 10 is far and away past the limit of human potential, so who knows at that point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2309,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","user":"Ashenrogue","date":". 04-05-2017, 12:18 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-05T02:45:57-07:00","id":1073644,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073264#post1073264\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Isn't that proposal? Just roll unskilled modifiers into the general situational modifers?<br /> <br /> That would even allow a degree of consistency in giving skill based modifers for other ratings aside from zero. I mean, to use Malus' example, someone trained in first aid, isn't going to be much more equiped for performing an operation than the unskilled person. I mean, as is, the core describes the various dot ratings in highly differentiated ways which a +1 mechanic doesn't really do.</div> </div> </div> </div>Someone who can perform a tracheostomy is far better prepared than someone who can't even perform a heimlich maneuver.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-05T04:11:30-07:00","id":1073661,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073553#post1073553\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sure, you'd have no chance of doing the super high technical aspects of the mental skills but that applies to the highest form of any of the skills. You'd have about as much luck performing a complex acrobatic routine as you would open heart surgery given no experience in Athletic activites and only a 5 minute explanation on proper form.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't actually consider the two equivalent within their respective skills, but... we should not neglect equipment bonuses. Mental skills have plentiful equipment bonuses. If I perform surgery in a hospital with staff and have relevant manuals then I'm up to a +4 or 5 bonus. I get +1 for appropriate attire during the acrobatics, not much else will help. Standard rules makes the two actions roughly as likely to be successful which is about right for the given situations. It would be much easier to perform the surgery if the untrained penalties were the same for all skills.<br /> <br /> Realism isn't the only consideration. Physical and social skills are good choices for every character. Some character types have little use for mental skills. Decreasing the incentive to buy at least a dot in a mental skill that a player might want to use increases the likelihood of some players feeling like they are wasting 4 points at character creation dumping them into mental skills when they probably don't need to do it. Then anyone that wants to play a character focusing on mental skills has less motivation because their character is actually balanced weaker than the physical and social types.<br /> <br /> It should be easy to see the benefit to buying a +4 bonus equivalent for that first dot of a mental skill, twice that of physical and social skills. All skills being equal makes mental skills less appealing. Their purview too often falls into the mundane, the parts of a story that are skipped. The system allows mental skills to be impressive on the occasions they are useful. I'm just not feeling the &quot;make them the same&quot; perspective.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-05T06:47:51-07:00","id":1073698,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073644#post1073644\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Someone who can perform a tracheostomy is far better prepared than someone who can't even perform a heimlich maneuver.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay. That's not really first aid, I mean it technically might classify as it, but first aid generally means people with very basic training. A tracheotomy probably shouldn't be done by someone with no formal medical training.<br /> <br /> Also, I don't think you are. It's not something you can just work out from general principles. A paramedic is going to fail at open heart surgery as much as anyone else; they're not trained to do it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-05T09:35:26-07:00","id":1073766,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Diggs</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073661#post1073661\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't actually consider the two equivalent within their respective skills, but... we should not neglect equipment bonuses. Mental skills have plentiful equipment bonuses. If I perform surgery in a hospital with staff and have relevant manuals then I'm up to a +4 or 5 bonus. I get +1 for appropriate attire during the acrobatics, not much else will help. Standard rules makes the two actions roughly as likely to be successful which is about right for the given situations. It would be much easier to perform the surgery if the untrained penalties were the same for all skills.<br /> <br /> Realism isn't the only consideration. Physical and social skills are good choices for every character. Some character types have little use for mental skills. Decreasing the incentive to buy at least a dot in a mental skill that a player might want to use increases the likelihood of some players feeling like they are wasting 4 points at character creation dumping them into mental skills when they probably don't need to do it. Then anyone that wants to play a character focusing on mental skills has less motivation because their character is actually balanced weaker than the physical and social types.<br /> <br /> It should be easy to see the benefit to buying a +4 bonus equivalent for that first dot of a mental skill, twice that of physical and social skills. All skills being equal makes mental skills less appealing. Their purview too often falls into the mundane, the parts of a story that are skipped. The system allows mental skills to be impressive on the occasions they are useful. I'm just not feeling the &quot;make them the same&quot; perspective.</div> </div> </div> </div>You could also have a proper instructor there giving you tips, and you can perform warm ups before the actual attempt, so you aren't completely without equipment bonuses. And being good choices doesn't mean that everyone has them. Athletics is good to have but I know a lot of people who haven't seriously exerted any physical effort in years. They <i>can</i> run, and they <i>can</i> jump, just like people who have no dots in Computers <i>can</i> use the basic functions of a computer, but mechanically they don't really have any dots in Athletics.<br /> <br /> Just like some characters have little use for Mental skills, some characters have little use for Physical or Social skills. A hacker and politician have little use for physical skills, a scientist and a brute might have little use for Social skills, and a gymnast or hired muscle might have little need for Mental skills. They all probably want a sprinkling of those skills anyway, for one thing or another, but they're tertiary to their other wants.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-05T11:49:41-07:00","id":1073812,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073644#post1073644\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Someone who can perform a tracheostomy is far better prepared than someone who can't even perform a heimlich maneuver.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing about CofD's Skill system is, the number of dots someone has in Medicine doesn't actually tell you if they can perform a tracheostomy or not, or if they can perform a heimlich maneuver or not. In theory, anyone can attempt anything unless the Storyteller specifically rules otherwise. Highly specialized tasks that can only be attempted by someone with the relevant training to do so are represented by the ST saying &quot;you must have a relavent Specialty to attempt this task&quot; or &quot;you must have at least X dots in the relevant Skill to attempt this task.&quot; In either case, untrained penalties aren't actually relevant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-05T16:09:29-07:00","id":1073917,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073766#post1073766\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You could also have a proper instructor there giving you tips, and you can perform warm ups before the actual attempt, so you aren't completely without equipment bonuses. And being good choices doesn't mean that everyone has them. Athletics is good to have but I know a lot of people who haven't seriously exerted any physical effort in years. They <i>can</i> run, and they <i>can</i> jump, just like people who have no dots in Computers <i>can</i> use the basic functions of a computer, but mechanically they don't really have any dots in Athletics.<br /> <br /> Just like some characters have little use for Mental skills, some characters have little use for Physical or Social skills. A hacker and politician have little use for physical skills, a scientist and a brute might have little use for Social skills, and a gymnast or hired muscle might have little need for Mental skills. They all probably want a sprinkling of those skills anyway, for one thing or another, but they're tertiary to their other wants.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm getting confused about whether you are talking about a game or simulating reality. Most of the game is social interaction and combat, so every player character needs skills allowing them to participate most of the time. There are less useful physical and social skills, but every mental skill falls in that group. No mental skill is as useful to a player as a combat skill or a talking skill. Mental skills can be as important, but not generally as useful to a fun game experience.<br /> <br /> There was talk of a hack which suggests to me a change in the dynamics of the game. Obviously, the developers thought mental skill actions would be more of the type that an average untrained person would have little chance of success. Physical and social actions would be more of the type that untrained people have a decent chance of success. I'm going to see less complex acrobatic routines than treating gunshots or stab wounds. If a group has an experience that differs then adjusting the standard penalty to reflect the kinds of actions that typically occur makes sense, but that seems like simply modifying the existing rules so they work as intended.<br /> <br /> There would be no significant change to say the standard penalty is -2 instead of -3 because most of the rolls should be getting a bonus for being easier actions in order to get the chance of success where it reasonably should be. If complex acrobatic routines are the typical physical action then I would increase the penalty. It would not be arbitrary. It would be shorthand for the penalty that should be added for that level of difficulty. That kind of rule change is a distinction without a difference. The rules would work exactly as now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T01:54:05-07:00","id":1074059,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Diggs</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073917#post1073917\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm getting confused about whether you are talking about a game or simulating reality. Most of the game is social interaction and combat, so every player character needs skills allowing them to participate most of the time. There are less useful physical and social skills, but every mental skill falls in that group. No mental skill is as useful to a player as a combat skill or a talking skill. Mental skills can be as important, but not generally as useful to a fun game experience.<br /> <br /> There was talk of a hack which suggests to me a change in the dynamics of the game. Obviously, the developers thought mental skill actions would be more of the type that an average untrained person would have little chance of success. Physical and social actions would be more of the type that untrained people have a decent chance of success. I'm going to see less complex acrobatic routines than treating gunshots or stab wounds. If a group has an experience that differs then adjusting the standard penalty to reflect the kinds of actions that typically occur makes sense, but that seems like simply modifying the existing rules so they work as intended.<br /> <br /> There would be no significant change to say the standard penalty is -2 instead of -3 because most of the rolls should be getting a bonus for being easier actions in order to get the chance of success where it reasonably should be. If complex acrobatic routines are the typical physical action then I would increase the penalty. It would not be arbitrary. It would be shorthand for the penalty that should be added for that level of difficulty. That kind of rule change is a distinction without a difference. The rules would work exactly as now.</div> </div> </div> </div>A little bit of both, really. And the game is what you make of it. If you aren't using Mental skills much it isn't that they are inherently devalued, it's just that you are at a particular table that doesn't focus that much on them.<br /> <br /> And I disagree that mental skills would be any harder untrained than physical or social skills. You can try to do them, sure, but assuming we're talking about the same level of use, you aren't really anymore likely to succeed at one without training than you would the other. Just giving it a random try I don't think you're anymore likely to fail at hopping a fence than you would treating a light injury or finding Joe Randoms poorly hidden porn stash on his computer.<br /> <br /> You're also going to be seeing less open heart surgeries or hacking into the pentagon than you are pickpocketing someone in the street or punching a guy in a bar. Long story short, if you're going to use a super specialized use of a Mental skill as an example you should compare it to a super specialized use of a Physical or Social skill.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2309,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","user":"Ashenrogue","date":". 04-06-2017, 04:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T02:41:42-07:00","id":1074065,"title":"","content":"I think one of the ways were the higher Mental untrained penalty can be removed and compensated for that hasn't been brought up is that Mental Skills lend themselves more readily to extended actions in a lot of basic applications (not that there aren't plenty of Physical/Social examples too, but in application I think there's a solid case for them to be less common). Being untrained is a massive penalty to extended actions because your maximum theoretical rolls are based on your unmodified Attribute + Skill + Specialty; even if you take out the dice penalties (not that I'm advocating this, I think the idea of standardizing them at -2 is better than most other proposed changes so far).<br /> <br /> Consider an Attribute 3/Skill 0 character, vs an Attribute 3/Skill 1/one applicable Specialty character (again, ignoring dice penalties).<br /> <br /> In a single roll it's 3 dice vs. 5 dice, which is a decent advantage, but not a ground shaking one.<br /> <br /> For an extended roll it's a maximum of 9 dice vs a maximum of 25 dice... which is a huge advantage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T02:42:43-07:00","id":1074066,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Diggs</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073917#post1073917\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Obviously, the developers thought mental skill actions would be more of the type that an average untrained person would have little chance of success.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't put a lot in that. I mean, it's a very small part of an elaborate system. It seems as likely that someone put it in and then no one has thought to question it.<br /> <br /> To speculate a bit more, the WoD system had division by traits(?) skills and knowledges, where the penalties increased gradually. It's noticable that most of the mental skills would have been in the knowledge catagory, so it seems plausible that someone looked at it and just assumed it made sense to carry a higher penalty over. It's possible no one's really given it much thought since the orginal Masquerade.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T03:43:30-07:00","id":1074080,"title":"","content":"Given that untrained penalties have been used as things to negate for things like splat bonuses, I don't think it's a safe assumption to say that it was one way back when and never looked at again.<br /> <br /> It's also very misleading to say that the WoD had penalties that increased gradually. The categories were, &quot;no penalty,&quot; &quot;minor penalty,&quot; and, &quot;you automatically fail if you don't have dots in this.&quot; which is a very rapid progression for only three data points.<br /> <br /> It's a lot a speculation, and frankly is based on a fairly uncharitable set of assumptions about the nWoD/CofD developers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T08:39:16-07:00","id":1074153,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It's a lot a speculation, and frankly is based on a fairly uncharitable set of assumptions about the nWoD/CofD developers. </div> </div> </div>It's only uncharitable if you have an unreasonable expectation of writers and developers. I didn't condemn it as bad or lazy, merely that I can see how a minor idea would simply get reused in a system without much examination. Especially as the system is itself a redesign of an older one, and build with many of the same ideas. I mean, if your baseline is that knowledges should be impossible unskilled, then a -3 could look like a fairly reasonable compromise.<br /> <br /> Also we're dealing with a mechanic baked into the system. There have only been two editions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T10:33:27-07:00","id":1074222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1074153#post1074153\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's only uncharitable if you have an unreasonable expectation of writers and developers.</div> </div> </div> </div>I fail to see how, &quot;thinking about the mechanics as they were developed or revised,&quot; is an unreasonable expectation of the developers (and I only said developers, not the writers in general). Thinking about the mechanics - even relatively minor ones - is part of a developer's job.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I didn't condemn it as bad or lazy, merely that I can see how a minor idea would simply get reused in a system without much examination. </div> </div> </div>So, you're not explicitly saying it was lazy, you're just speculating that it was lazy. I'm not seeing the difference in what the end point is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mean, if your baseline is that knowledges should be impossible unskilled, then a -3 could look like a fairly reasonable compromise. </div> </div> </div>Which is, of course, pretty much the opposite of what your assumptions and speculations would suggest. You can't reach a compromise solution to &quot;what to do with untrained penalties in this new system since we changed how Skills are categorize,&quot; without actually examining it with some actual thought to the impact of different options on the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also we're dealing with a mechanic baked into the system. </div> </div> </div>What does this mean beyond, &quot;this mechanic is in the system?&quot;<br /> <br /> Usually &quot;baked in,&quot; means there's a significant amount of additional design around the concept such that you can't remove it without more work than just starting over. Something like the TN for dice being 8 is baked in, because if you changed it to 7, it would have significant knock on effects on the assumptions of almost every mechanic in the game because it alters the fundamental math of the dice odds.<br /> <br /> While removing untrained penalties completely would impact a small set of bonuses/penalties that specifically interact with them, and would have some impact on Contested (or semi-Contested things like full combat) simply by making untrained and trained a bit closer, I cannot think of a single mechanic that is significantly altered if you make the untrained penalty a flat -X for all Skills instead of Mental Skills having a higher one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T17:55:45-07:00","id":1074429,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1073812#post1073812\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The thing about CofD's Skill system is, the number of dots someone has in Medicine doesn't actually tell you if they can perform a tracheostomy or not, or if they can perform a heimlich maneuver or not. In theory, anyone can attempt anything unless the Storyteller specifically rules otherwise. Highly specialized tasks that can only be attempted by someone with the relevant training to do so are represented by the ST saying &quot;you must have a relavent Specialty to attempt this task&quot; or &quot;you must have at least X dots in the relevant Skill to attempt this task.&quot; In either case, untrained penalties aren't actually relevant.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you don't have 1 dot in medicine, you can't even attempt to gain the required specialty.<br /> <br /> That helicopter specialty on drive? It would take at least 6 months to get.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T18:40:42-07:00","id":1074449,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1074429#post1074429\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If you don't have 1 dot in medicine, you can't even attempt to gain the required specialty.<br /> <br /> That helicopter specialty on drive? It would take at least 6 months to get.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, and in both cases the -1 to -3 for being untrained have no bearing on the results of an attempt to perform the action in question.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T19:56:09-07:00","id":1074477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1074066#post1074066\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I wouldn't put a lot in that. I mean, it's a very small part of an elaborate system. It seems as likely that someone put it in and then no one has thought to question it.<br /> <br /> To speculate a bit more, the WoD system had division by traits(?) skills and knowledges, where the penalties increased gradually. It's noticable that most of the mental skills would have been in the knowledge catagory, so it seems plausible that someone looked at it and just assumed it made sense to carry a higher penalty over. It's possible no one's really given it much thought since the orginal Masquerade.</div> </div> </div> </div>You suggest that I consider it as likely that developers decided to arbitrarily create rules without any thought towards what the rules meant as the developers set out to create rules to make a quality game, aka their job. You're on your own with that, buddy. I'm giving them credit that the developers considered every rule carefully and any issues would be a result of an inability to convey their thoughts via text. It is unreasonable and unfair to work under the assumption that the developers did not intend for the average mental skill action roll to represent more complicated activities than the average physical or social skill action roll when it comes to untrained characters. Why? Because the penalties are significantly different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T20:31:02-07:00","id":1074493,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1074059#post1074059\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A little bit of both, really. And the game is what you make of it. If you aren't using Mental skills much it isn't that they are inherently devalued, it's just that you are at a particular table that doesn't focus that much on them.<br /> <br /> And I disagree that mental skills would be any harder untrained than physical or social skills. You can try to do them, sure, but assuming we're talking about the same level of use, you aren't really anymore likely to succeed at one without training than you would the other. Just giving it a random try I don't think you're anymore likely to fail at hopping a fence than you would treating a light injury or finding Joe Randoms poorly hidden porn stash on his computer.<br /> <br /> You're also going to be seeing less open heart surgeries or hacking into the pentagon than you are pickpocketing someone in the street or punching a guy in a bar. Long story short, if you're going to use a super specialized use of a Mental skill as an example you should compare it to a super specialized use of a Physical or Social skill.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're so far out into left field I don't even know if I can take this on. We are talking about the rules for a game. The rules abstractly create a simulation only to provide a gaming environment. It is invalid to take the position that the rules should simulate reality in order to simulate reality. The rules are always about the game, solely and entirely. Anyone that disagrees and wants to take another position... we are at an en passe. <br /> <br /> We aren't talking about the same level of use. The rules never talked about the same level of use. There are a few levels; automatic failure, automatic success, simple untrained and difficult untrained. The default for mental actions is difficult while social and physical is simple. Look at the sample actions provided. It isn't about hacking the pentagon. We are talking about hacking computers, programming or database searches and contrasting that with hitting something or picking a pocket. The first group requires knowledge to succeed without blind luck. <br /> <br /> Long story short, it is an invalid comparison, super specialized to super specialized, as you put it. The only proper comparison is typical roll versus typical roll (because it is a game rather than a pure simulation). We must neglect all the possible rolls that we would consider unnecessary to roll. What we are left with is frequent and infrequent. To me, you want to compare infrequent physical actions to frequent mental actions or infrequent mental actions to frequent physical actions. The problem is they are not the same level of difficulty for me, or the people that wrote the rules. Why on earth would the penalty be the same for different difficulties? If the difficulties are the same at your table, which has yet to be asserted by anyone, I already said the penalty should be adjusted to match the typical action.<br /> <br /> The premise that a hack is needed seems to be flawed. If the penalty is frequently too severe then you change the penalty. That is within the rules and how the set penalty was created. When the developer table is different than your table, make the penalty appropriate. The current rule system does not have an arbitrary penalty. The roll gets adjustments from other rules and should end up where it should be. Actions that are atypically easy get bonuses, difficult get penalties. -3 is for difficult acts which describes most of the sample actions for mental skills. Less difficult acts like the most examples for physical and social skills get a -1. Does that not make sense?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-06T23:25:21-07:00","id":1074534,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Diggs</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1074493#post1074493\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You're so far out into left field I don't even know if I can take this on. We are talking about the rules for a game. The rules abstractly create a simulation only to provide a gaming environment. It is invalid to take the position that the rules should simulate reality in order to simulate reality. The rules are always about the game, solely and entirely. Anyone that disagrees and wants to take another position... we are at an en passe.<br /> <br /> We aren't talking about the same level of use. The rules never talked about the same level of use. There are a few levels; automatic failure, automatic success, simple untrained and difficult untrained. The default for mental actions is difficult while social and physical is simple. Look at the sample actions provided. It isn't about hacking the pentagon. We are talking about hacking computers, programming or database searches and contrasting that with hitting something or picking a pocket. The first group requires knowledge to succeed without blind luck.<br /> <br /> Long story short, it is an invalid comparison, super specialized to super specialized, as you put it. The only proper comparison is typical roll versus typical roll (because it is a game rather than a pure simulation). We must neglect all the possible rolls that we would consider unnecessary to roll. What we are left with is frequent and infrequent. To me, you want to compare infrequent physical actions to frequent mental actions or infrequent mental actions to frequent physical actions. The problem is they are not the same level of difficulty for me, or the people that wrote the rules. Why on earth would the penalty be the same for different difficulties? If the difficulties are the same at your table, which has yet to be asserted by anyone, I already said the penalty should be adjusted to match the typical action.<br /> <br /> The premise that a hack is needed seems to be flawed. If the penalty is frequently too severe then you change the penalty. That is within the rules and how the set penalty was created. When the developer table is different than your table, make the penalty appropriate. The current rule system does not have an arbitrary penalty. The roll gets adjustments from other rules and should end up where it should be. Actions that are atypically easy get bonuses, difficult get penalties. -3 is for difficult acts which describes most of the sample actions for mental skills. Less difficult acts like the most examples for physical and social skills get a -1. Does that not make sense?</div> </div> </div> </div>It seems you aren't quite getting what I'm trying to get at so I'll try to break this down a bit. In your original post you stated that you could perform any of the physical or social skills without any training with a few minutes of instruction. You then went on to say that even with 10 Intelligence you wouldn't be able to do the same with Mental skills, listing the likes of <i><b>open heart surgery</b></i> as your example as to why you couldn't do so with a Mental skill. That reasoning is <i>greatly flawed</i>. So much so that I had to point out that if you were trying to use <i>that</i> kind of metric you should compare highly technical uses of a skill (open heart surgery) to a highly technical use of a physical or social skill (high level acrobatics being my example, with which you wouldn't be able to perform any better than open heart surgery given no training). That was the totality of my argument here. That the reasoning you were using to seemingly use as an excuse for Mental skills to have a higher untrained penalty was flawed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T03:46:45-07:00","id":1074577,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1074449#post1074449\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, and in both cases the -1 to -3 for being untrained have no bearing on the results of an attempt to perform the action in question.</div> </div> </div> </div>You think rolling chance-dies bears no correlation on the attempt? Alrighty then...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-07T05:41:58-07:00","id":1074605,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1072953-hacking-untrained-penalties?p=1074577#post1074577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You think rolling chance-dies bears no correlation on the attempt? Alrighty then...</div> </div> </div> </div>You might need to go back and re-read my post we're discussing if you think chance dice are involved. If the Storyteller says &quot;you can't attempt this action without a relevant Specialty&quot; then you don't get a chance die unless you have the Specialty. And you can't have the Specialty and have an untrained penalty, because you need at least a dot in the associated Skill to have a Specialty in it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}